**Definition 17.1 (boundary rejection criteria).** A lowering of §§11–16
fails its specification if any of the following occurs: a self copy/cons/
tail reads a newly written destination as its source; the bank's measured
base is taken after construction; a dry length includes F or omits sep;
its parity flips before the descriptor seal; an input bit is tested through
a latch overwritten before WR; a scratch import moves its own source head;
a length routine reads and appends on one ordinary head; a channel uses
a staged descriptor's wire length as its payload length; a bootstrap uses
the capture controller before installing it; a replay of activation looks
up only the old source Ready row; a zero wire target simulates one row;
a meter receipt restores an old rather than the just-updated read head;
a loader installs archival framing as though it were native code; a
bootstrap enables its partial table before DONE; an unretained optional
output choice changes the history; or a copied destination becomes
available before its final seal. Import also fails its representation if
the initial port-37 F becomes a field, a legitimate empty field is skipped,
the final head-table field loses its delimiter, the old-frame terminal 0
changes another field's index, or any helper moves head 37 while it is
the import source. The old
falsifiers in Definition 9.1 also apply.

These conditions distinguish executable realization from mathematical
encoding. The declared bounded bootstrap permission is an instrument
premise; a proof for the old unextended menu must not silently use it.
Its absence cannot be repaired by a larger interpreter or more replay.

